Date:     Fri, 12 Feb 93 9:33:18 GMT
From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject: LAW&ECON: Harm & Certainty

The notion of legal offenses that I'm operating on is that what's concerned
are harms to others.  If A accidentally harms B, A has committed a tort
against B.  If A intentionally harms B, A has committed a crime against B.
Viewing these as costs that A has externalized onto B, and assuming that
the cost is identical in both cases, I'd argue that the amount of restitution
owed to the plaintiff/victim by the offender is more for the crime than for
the tort.  For the tort, it would be the amount of the externalized cost
plus the cost of collecting the fine.  For the crime, it would be these
plus an additional factor (monetary or not) which would be to deny the
offender any benefit obtained from the crime and thus deter recidivism.

I'm elaborating this at such a length because I'm still learning this stuff,
and invite correction if I'm wrong about anything.

However, going by the above, I can't see how there's any more burden of
proof to show that B has no claim against A for harm A inflicts upon B
by smoking... that is, that A has no overriding right to smoke, than there
is any burden of proof to show that A has no overriding right to tresspass
upon the land of B.

Tom, you seem to have gotten the presumption backwards.  Surely the burden
of proof would be on the defendant to show he had such an overriding right,
not upon the plaintiff to show that he didn't!  If not, why not?

In the case of a smoker allegedly harming another, the presumptions are
that there are no contractual agreements between the parties to the effect
that A waives rights to redress from B's smoking, and that people have the
right not to be harmed by others, at least prima facie.  I don't see why
this wouldn't hold for some plaintiffs/victims merely because of their
age or dependency on the offender.

As for the argument about the justification of statism, Tom robs statism
of its meaning.  It's nonsense to say that if A and B both support statism
then they support the coercion of each other - they both consent to their
treatment, contradicting the possiblity of their being coerced.  Consent
and coercion are contradictory and antithetical.  There is no coercion
where there is consent and there is no consent where there is coercion.

Statism doesn't mean any particular governing institution that people may
be subjects or citizens of.  It means the systematic usurpation of the
autonomy of individual people.  It means coercion.

Tom's approach may be useful in discussing the justification of Institution
X, but not statism.  In regard to Institution X, one can say that those
who accept its authority are subject to it, and that those who don't aren't.
It would be collectivist to say that Institution X is completely unjustified
for all those it claims as its subjects merely because one, a few, or even
many of them reject it.  For those who do accept it, it's justified.

But, this justification is only insofar as Institution X excercises authority
exclusively over those who have accepted its authority and not over those
who have rejected it.

Lastly, Dale Worley is attacking the certainty of prediction in the science
of economics.  After failing to get a response out of him the first time I
called him on such a claim, I now challenge him to a debate:

RESOLVED: It is possible to make predictions with certainty in economics.

Dale seems to think that the fact that economics differs from physics makes
this true.  Non-sequitur.

We don't need to run controlled experiments in order to deduce conclusions
from, e.g., the action axiom, that have the certainty of euclidean geometry.
And a good thing, too, because we can't!

If the above resolution isn't acceptable, I propose an alternate:

RESOLVED: Completely freeing up the market of the USA will increase the
average income levels and the income distribution will be less disparate
than it is now.

Tim Starr - Renaissance Now! - Think Universally, Act Selfishly
starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu

"True greatness consists in the use of a powerful understanding to enlighten
oneself and others." - Francois Marie Arouet Voltaire

