Date: Fri, 12 Feb 93 10:47:41 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: LAW&ECON: Harm & Certainty

Tim Starr suggests dividing torts from crimes along lines of intention. 
Regardless of whether or not this is a good idea, this just is not how it
is done at present.  Nor did common law ever draw the line that way.

Tim writes:

>The notion of legal offenses that I'm operating on is that what's concerned
>are harms to others.  If A accidentally harms B, A has committed a tort
>against B.  

But as I noted earlier, assault and battery are both torts and crimes.  A
tort can be intentional.

>If A intentionally harms B, A has committed a crime against B.

Ever hear of criminal negligence?

>I'm elaborating this at such a length because I'm still learning this stuff,
>and invite correction if I'm wrong about anything.

Well, you *did* ask!  8-)  My own take on the subject:  Junk criminal law
altogether.  It is an entirely pathological area of law, constituted of bad
ideas built on false assumptions.  Tort law has tools enought to deal with
intentional, harmful acts.

>However, going by the above, I can't see how there's any more burden of
>proof to show that B has no claim against A for harm A inflicts upon B
>by smoking... 

I was arguing not as a matter of theory, but as of fact, that smoking most
always occurs in situtations where the "smokee" has waived the right to sue
by entering another person's property with notice of the risk of smoke.

>Tom, you seem to have gotten the presumption backwards.  Surely the burden
>of proof would be on the defendant to show he had such an overriding right,
>not upon the plaintiff to show that he didn't!  If not, why not?

Plaintiff bears the initial burden of proof.  Defendant can mount an
"affirmative defense" that plaintiff waived the right to escape smoke by
entering defendant's property.  The burden of proof could go either way
here, though I agree that it should probably fall on defendant.  This would
be easy to meet, however:  "I own the restaurant and I have a sign at the
door saying that smoking is allowed."  Boom!  A title document and a photo
of the entryway puts the ball back in plaintiff's court.  

>In the case of a smoker allegedly harming another, the presumptions are
>that there are no contractual agreements between the parties to the effect
>that A waives rights to redress from B's smoking, and that people have the
>right not to be harmed by others, at least prima facie.  

But who is harming who here?  I suggest that you read Coase's famous
article "The Problem of Social Cost."  It argues, in effect, that both
tortfeasor and victim are equally to blame.  How we allocate liability
makes no difference, in terms of allocative efficiency, in a world without
transaction costs.  This is the famous Coase's Theorem, and you simply
cannot compare liability regimes intelligently without taking it into
account.

>As for the argument about the justification of statism, Tom robs statism
>of its meaning.  It's nonsense to say that if A and B both support statism
>then they support the coercion of each other - they both consent to their
>treatment, contradicting the possiblity of their being coerced.  

Ah!  Now Tim is getting to the heart of the matter.  I both agree and
disagree.  I agree that statism implies coercing nonconsenting parties
because I recognize that my justification argument will help statists to
recognize that they have been grasping for more than they can ask for.  I
honestly feel that many statists do not recognize the coercive nature of
their actions.  They assume that all citizens have consented to the
institution in question.  I have designed my argument to sound appealing to
such statists--they want to think their institutions have won the consent
of the people--and yet to lead them to recognize that they cannot justify
coercing those of us who withold consent.  So I agree with Tim's
description insofar as it describes my strategy of moving statists from one
conception of their actions to another.

I disagree with Tim's description, however, insofar as it denies that some
people do indeed consent to statist institutions.  Political masochism is
very real.  Now, you may claim that these people are not being coerced, but
that does not tell us how to label the same institution that does coerce
others.  One option, which Tim seems to embrace, is to say that we should
not label institutions but only institutional relations:

>Statism doesn't mean any particular governing institution that people may
>be subjects or citizens of.  It means the systematic usurpation of the
>autonomy of individual people.  It means coercion.

This sounds agreeable enough (except that statism does not "mean" coercion,
strictly speaking).  But I think that by now we are only choosing between
different approaches to discussing statism.  I argue that my approach is
preferable on the grounds that is more accurate and more apt to persuade.

>Tom's approach may be useful in discussing the justification of Institution
>X, but not statism.  In regard to Institution X, one can say that those
>who accept its authority are subject to it, and that those who don't aren't.
>It would be collectivist to say that Institution X is completely unjustified
>for all those it claims as its subjects merely because one, a few, or even
>many of them reject it.  For those who do accept it, it's justified.
>
>But, this justification is only insofar as Institution X excercises authority
>exclusively over those who have accepted its authority and not over those
>who have rejected it.

Tim has done a very good job of describing my view of justification.  But
to do it justice, one needs also to note that my view of justification ends
up strongly condemning statism even apart from institutions.  It does not
take the blunt approach favored by most libertarians: "Statism is
unjustified,"; instead it takes a more subtle approach:  "This is a
reasonable definition of justification, now isn't it?  And by its terms
many institutions commonly called "statist" are justified with respect to
many people.  Isn't that comforting?  But please take note that these same
institutions are not justified with respect to everyone."  This gets us to
the same point, but does so more persuasively, I think.  It defines the
relevant term (justification), establishes common ground, and distinguishes
between the alternatives.  It still gets us everything we want,
rhetorically speaking:  it still tells statists to get off our backs and
let us choose our own governing institutions.  

I also think that my approach is more accurate.  Some institutions do
operate on statist principles.  Yet these same institutions may be
justified with respect to some people.  It is thus entirely accurate to say
that such statist institutions can be justified.

Tim has made an important distinction, however.  My argument does not
justify statism per se  In fact, the way I define justification precludes
applying it to an abstract doctrine.  We can only consent to particular
applications of doctrines.  Now, we may feel compelled to say something
about statism, and I think we should.  But we should only say it is
inefficient, or that it encourages immoral behavior.  But I don't think we
should say it is justified or unjustified.  In order to support the
methodologically individualism that is most consistent with classical
liberalism, we should only apply justification with respect to the choices
that particular individuals make about particular institutions. 
Justification, unlike efficiency or morality, is a practical, real-world
notion.  

Here's one way to think about the choice I am posing:  My approach demands,
in effect, the privatisation of justification!  The traditional libertarian
approach is more akin to a struggle for monopolistic control of which
ideologies merit the label "justified."  It's either them or us, under this
traditional approach, either statism or anarchism.  I say we should let
individuals decide for themselves what ideologies they like, and that we
should measure their choices by whether or not they consent to particular
institutional arrangements that embody those ideologies.  

'Nuff said.  I gotta' pack for a trip out of town.  My thanks for Tim to
intelligently probing my arguments.

 

