Date: Fri, 12 Feb 93 09:07:08 -0800
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: VR: Topologies of virtual worlds

Derek Zahn comments on Anton Sherwood's ideas about VR:

>Another interesting issue is whether objects in my segment could be
>protected cryptographically -- that is, unless an incoming ray
>had a proper encryption key attached, protected objects would
>appear invisible (oxymoron though that is).  I don't know very much
>about cryptography; could such a system be relatively secure?  Tim?
>
>Thanks, Anton, for your delightful speculations on VR geometry.

I, too, enjoyed Anton's article (but I didn't take notes...). A couple of
points:

1. Crypto will be the _building material_ for walls, doors, locks, and the
very structure of cyberspace. Since nearly "anything is possible" in
VR/cyberspace, solidity and access control _must_ come through the tools of
cryptography (these are basically the only tools we have--understand that
by cryptography I mean all aspects of computer security, access control,
password schemes, end-to-end encryption, digital money, digital
time-stamping, etc.).

2. The "Metaverse" of Stephenson's "Snow Crash" is delightful, as fiction,
but is artificially constrained, as Anton noted. The spherical arrangement,
the "meridian"-like highways criss-crossing the Metavers are baloney.

Sure, _some_ cyberspaces may have these. By "some" I mean to  emphasize the
market aspects: cyberspaces will be built and colonized in all kinds of
ways, with non-Euclidean geometries, non-compact spaces (!!!), oddball
topologies, "growing like Topsy" spaces, and so on. Access to these
abstract spaces (data bases, if you like) will be mediated by cryptographic
means, natch, and will be market-based.

The most successful "spaces," like theme parks or restaurants or whatever,
will thrive and grow.

-Tim


