Subject: NANO: mining the backyard

   From: D Anton Sherwood <dasher@well.sf.ca.us>

   I seem to remember that (at room temperature) silver is the best known
   conductor by cross-section, copper is next-best, and aluminum is best by
   mass.  Is that right?

   So why don't we see more aluminum wires?  Corrosion, ductility, price?

Aluminum forms an oxide skin that is non-conductive, which if it isn't
scraped off, causes other conductors to make rather poor contact with
it.  The bad contacts have resistance and generate heat.  In the long
run, aluminum house wiring has a tendency to cause houses to catch
fire, so it was banned.  This happened sometime in the 1970s, I think.

Dale

